{=============================================================================
  Maximum work delivered by a heat engine between two air tanks
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0057
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Two rigid vessels, each holding 30 kg of air at 900 K and 300 K respectively,
  are connected by a heat engine that takes heat from the hot vessel, delivers
  it to the cold one and produces work. The model computes the maximum work
  (reversible operation) and the common final temperature of the two vessels.

  The system is the extended system (engine + both vessels); the work of the
  engine stays inside it. Entropy balance of a closed system with no mass and no
  work exchange: S2-S1 = S_gen, i.e. DELTA_S = 0 for a reversible process, and
  the entropy change of the engine over one cycle is zero. Each vessel being
  rigid (p dv = 0), T ds = du = cv dT, hence (S-S_0) = m cv ln(T/T_0).

  Inputs : T_1a, T_1b (initial vessel temperatures), m_air (air mass per vessel)
  Outputs: W_max (maximum work), T_2 (common final temperature), Q_source,
           Q_sink (heat exchanged with each vessel), eta_max (W_max/Q_source)
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège, course Thermodynamique appliquée (MECA0002), repetition
           session R10, exercise 3 (2022-2023) - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"!Initial state of the two vessels"
T_1a = 626.85 [C]   "initial temperature of the hot vessel [°C], 900 K in the original"
T_1b = 26.85 [C]    "initial temperature of the cold vessel [°C], 300 K in the original"
m_air = 30 [kg]     "mass of air in each vessel [kg]"

"!Entropy balance of the extended system (engine + both vessels)"
"!S2-S1 = S_gen (= 0 for a reversible system) ==> DELTA_S = 0, isolated reversible system"
DELTA_S_1 + DELTA_S_2 + DELTA_S_heat_engine = 0   "entropy change of the extended system [J/K]"
DELTA_S_heat_engine = 0   "entropy change over a cycle of the engine is zero [J/K]"

"!Entropy change of each vessel: m cv ln(T/T_0); the temperatures are absolute"
"!inside the logarithm (as in the original, written in K)"
DELTA_S_1 = m_air*cv_air*ln((T_1a+273.15)/(T_2+273.15))   "entropy change of the hot vessel [J/K]"
DELTA_S_2 = m_air*cv_air*ln((T_1b+273.15)/(T_2+273.15))   "entropy change of the cold vessel [J/K]"

"!cv of air, taken constant and evaluated at the intermediate temperature T_2"
"!as in the original: the ambient temperature could have been used as well, which"
"!does not really affect the results"
cv_air = cv(Air, T=T_2)   "constant specific heat of air [J/kg-K]"

"!Both vessels are stabilised at T_2 at the end of the process"
Q_source = m_air*cv_air*(T_2-T_1a)   "heat given by the hot vessel to the engine [J], negative"
Q_sink = m_air*cv_air*(T_2-T_1b)     "heat received by the cold vessel from the engine [J], positive"

W_max = -(abs(Q_source)-abs(Q_sink))  "maximum work, as written in the original (the work delivered by the engine is the magnitude of this value) [J]"
eta_max = W_max/Q_source               "ratio W_max/Q_source, called the second-law efficiency in the original; positive, both terms being negative [-]"

"!Post-processing: magnitude of the maximum work (the engine delivers this work)"
W_max_delivered = abs(Q_source)-abs(Q_sink)   "maximum work delivered by the engine [J]"

"!Complementary explanation, as in the original:"
"!In a purely thermal exchange between the two vessels (no losses and no heat"
"!engine, hence no work produced) the energy balance alone would give"
"!Q_a = m_air*cv_air*(T_1a-T2_bis) = Q_b = m_air*cv_air*(T2_bis-T_1b)"
"!<=> T2_bis = (T_1a+T_1b)/2"
"!The entropy change along that transformation (which is not reversible, so the"
"!entropy changes are not equal) would follow the same relation m_air*cv_air*ln(T_1a/T2_bis),"
"!except that T2_bis differs from T_2 (the temperature computed with the heat engine)"
"!With the heat engine, producing work requires abs(Q_source) > abs(Q_sink), so the final"
"!equilibrium temperature is lower than the average of the two vessels (T2_bis)"
"!The entropy change still follows the same relation m_air*cv_air*ln(T_1a/T_2), but at"
"!another equilibrium temperature, and it is the reversibility condition that imposes"
"!that the entropy changes of the two vessels are equal (the engine matters little: the"
"!aim is the maximum work when the transformation is reversible)"